# Dense code and signal

## The request this skill answers

Strip padding: comments that inflate the line count, code that is never executed,
old experiments, defensive branches for cases that cannot happen, abstractions
that wrap nothing. Leave only the code.

That goal is right, and one common reading of it is wrong in a way that damages
the codebase. **The target is not "no comments".** The target is *no comments that
restate the code*. Those two rules are opposites, and applying the wrong one
produces code that is harder to change than the bloated version it replaced.

## The distinction that carries the whole skill

```
Delete:  a comment that repeats what the code says.
          // increment i by 1
          i++

Keep:    a comment that records something the code cannot say.
          // Off-by-one here is load-bearing: the caller relies on the
          // sentinel being returned rather than thrown. See issue #4471.
```

A comment earns its place only if deleting it would lose information. Test it by
covering the code: if the comment is still true and still needed, it stays. If
the rest of the code explains it, it goes.

The failure mode is asymmetric and worth naming. A redundant comment costs a
line to maintain and misleads nobody much. A deleted comment explaining a
non-obvious invariant looks like obvious code to the next reader, who changes
it, and breaks the invariant silently. **The first is a cost; the second is an
outage.** Aggressive deletion without judgement trades a visible annoyance for
an invisible failure.

Comment *why*, not *what*. "Why" cannot be recovered from the code; "what" always can.

## What to delete

| Pattern | Why it goes |
| --- | --- |
| Comment restating the next line | The code says it, and says it more accurately |
| Commented-out code | Not executed, not tested, and rots faster than code because nobody notices when it becomes wrong |
| Debug logging left behind | Costs a reader nothing to skip, costs production a lot |
| Unused imports, variables, parameters | No runtime meaning, constant review noise |
| Unreachable branches after an early return | Signals confusion about control flow |
| Dead feature flag with no callers | Looks live to everyone reading it |
| Wrapper that only forwards | An extra name to hold in your head for zero abstraction |
| Helper called exactly once, naming nothing | Indirection with no payoff |
| `if (x == true)` | Says less than `if (x)` and occupies the same space |
| Defensive branch for an impossible case | Untestable, therefore unverified, therefore a lie of safety |

The single most common find in a real review is a helper used once. It was
extracted to "keep the function short", and the function it came from was fine.
Extraction has a cost — a name to invent, a place to jump to — and it only pays
back when the same logic appears again.

## What to keep, and stop deleting

- The comment that explains **why** an apparently wrong line exists
- The invariant a caller must not break
- The reason a constant has its value, when the value is not derivable
- The workaround and its reference, so it is not "cleaned up" later
- Tests, including the ones that look obvious
- Error messages, which are code for the person who will read them at 3am

If you find yourself deleting the last comment in a function, that is a signal,
not a win. Ask whether the function's *shape* is what made it need explaining.
A function with five branches and a subtle ordering usually wants a redesign, not
a deletion.

## Measure density, do not eyeball it

Line count is not the measure. A 200-line function of dense logic and a 60-line
function with four comment blocks can have the same signal density, and the
second is often clearer.

What is worth counting:

```
unused symbols        functions, imports, variables with no reference
comment-to-code ratio comments that add information, not comments total
abstraction depth     call-stack layers between the caller and the real work
branch coverage       paths that exist but are never taken
```

The number that catches the most real waste is **abstraction depth**: how many
hops it takes to reach the code that does the thing. Depth three means every
debug session pays a tax.

## The removal order

Deletion in the wrong order wastes effort, because polishing prose around dead
code produces beautifully written dead code.

1. Remove what is not executed.
2. Remove what restates code.
3. Remove indirection that names nothing.
4. Collapse duplicated branches that differ only in a constant.
5. Merge near-identical functions whose parameters differ only by name.
6. Then reassess. Often the code is now short enough that the comment was never
   needed.

## Anti-rationalisations

| Excuse | Reality |
| --- | --- |
| "Comments are noise." | Restating the code is noise. Recording the decision is not. |
| "It is self-explanatory." | Self-explanatory means nobody had to write down the invariant. Who decided that? |
| "This branch can never happen." | Then delete it or assert it. Untestable safety is not safety. |
| "The helper improves readability." | Name what it names. If nothing, it is an extra hop. |
| "I will add a comment to explain it." | Then the code was unclear, not the comment. |
| "Removing lines makes it better." | Removing *signal-free* lines makes it better. Removing the rest makes it fragile. |
| "Coverage is 100%, so it is all used." | Coverage is execution, not intent. A defensive branch can be fully covered and still unreachable in reality. |
